(ns kura.bugyo.pricing
  "What a customer pays, what a node earns, and the gap between them.

  **Two bases, and confusing them reads the business backwards.**

  - The *wholesale* rate card — `storage-price-usd-tb-month` and everything
    above `versus-storj` — prices capacity per terabyte against what a node is
    paid to hold it. That is the number to compare with Storj, and the number
    a third-party node operator is quoted against.
  - The *retail* plane — `default-tiers` and `tier-economics` below — sells a
    quota at a subscription price, the way `kotobase.net` does. Its price is
    not anchored to $/TB at all, so the storage multiplier that dominates the
    wholesale card is a rounding error here.

  A B2-backed fleet is loss-making on the first basis and comfortably
  profitable on the second, at the same instant, on the same bytes
  (ADR-2607299200 section 6g). Every report that quotes a margin says which
  basis it is on.

  **Priced in USD, settled in USDC. No network token** (ADR-2607299200
  section 6a). A token whose value derives from network fees makes the
  security budget reflexive — collateral is worth least exactly when the
  network is being doubted — and puts every customer through an exchange to
  buy storage. Storj prices in USD for the same reason. A token is a
  fundraising instrument, not a protocol requirement.

  **The identity that makes the erasure code a business decision:**

      customer pays for LOGICAL bytes
      nodes are paid for PHYSICAL bytes
      the storage multiplier is the coordinator's cost of goods

  At the launch multiplier of 2.0x, one logical TB costs two physical TB of
  node payouts. Phase 0's job is to measure the node-loss rate that lets the
  code tighten to 1.625x, and that 19% reduction in COGS is a price cut, not
  margin — announced as one, because a network that pockets its own
  efficiency gains has no story for why anyone should help it measure them.

  Every rate here is a parameter with a documented default, not a constant.
  The defaults are modelled, not observed; `:provenance` says so on each one,
  and Phase 0 replaces them with measurements."
  (:require [erasure.lrc :as lrc]))

(def default-rates
  "What a node earns, and what the coordinator keeps.

  The two node rates were chosen as working figures and later checked against
  Storj's published storage-node payout schedule (storj.dev/node/payouts,
  read 2026-07-29): **$1.50/TB-month storage and $2.00/TB egress — both
  identical to these**, and Storj pays audit/repair bandwidth at the same
  $2.00/TB. So they are `:benchmarked`, not `:modelled`: they are what the
  incumbent publicly pays, which is a far stronger footing than a guess and
  still not a measurement of *our own* node economics. Phase 0 replaces them
  with observed figures; until then, matching the incumbent is the honest
  claim.

  A caution that cost real time to establish: a search summary reported
  Storj's egress payout as $20/TB and repair as $10/TB, which would have made
  the collateral model's repair anchor five times larger. The official payout
  page says $2.00 for both. Rates that move the bond get read from the source,
  not from a summary.

  `:margin` and `:epoch-days` remain choices, not observations."
  {:node-storage-usd-tb-month
   {:value 1.50 :provenance :benchmarked
    :source "storj.dev/node/payouts, read 2026-07-29 — Storj pays $1.50/TB-month"}
   :node-egress-usd-tb
   {:value 2.00 :provenance :benchmarked
    :source "storj.dev/node/payouts, read 2026-07-29 — Storj pays $2.00/TB egress and $2.00/TB audit/repair"}
   :margin {:value 0.35 :provenance :chosen}
   :epoch-days {:value 30 :provenance :chosen}})

(defn rates
  "Build a rate card, overriding any default."
  [overrides]
  (reduce-kv (fn [m k v] (assoc m k (if (map? v) v {:value v :provenance :supplied})))
             default-rates
             overrides))

(defn- v [rates k] (get-in rates [k :value]))

(defn multiplier
  "Physical bytes per logical byte for a layout — the COGS ratio."
  [layout]
  (/ (double (:n layout)) (:k layout)))

;; --- customer price --------------------------------------------------------

(defn storage-price-usd-tb-month
  "What a customer pays per LOGICAL TB-month.

  `node-cost x multiplier` is the floor; margin is applied on top. Stating it
  this way rather than as a single number means a change to the code's
  parameters shows up in the price automatically, which is the whole point of
  tracking the multiplier as a live value."
  [rates layout]
  (let [cogs (* (v rates :node-storage-usd-tb-month) (multiplier layout))]
    (/ cogs (- 1.0 (v rates :margin)))))

(defn egress-price-usd-tb
  "What a customer pays per TB read.

  Egress is NOT multiplied by the code: a systematic read fetches k data
  shards and reconstructs nothing, so serving one logical TB moves one
  physical TB (ADR section 7). A network that charged egress at the storage
  multiplier would be billing for reconstruction it does not perform."
  [rates]
  (/ (v rates :node-egress-usd-tb) (- 1.0 (v rates :margin))))

(def storj-reference
  "The incumbent, for comparison. Retail from storj.io/pricing (Standard tier,
  read 2026-07-29); expansion factor from the publicly documented 29-of-80
  erasure share set.

  The comparison that matters is not the retail number but where it comes
  from: Storj's cost of goods is 2.76x its node rate, so at $7.00 retail it
  keeps ~41%. kura's launch configuration keeps ~35% and still lands ~34%
  cheaper, because the difference is the storage multiplier and nothing else.
  Node operators are paid the same either way — this does not undercut them."
  {:retail-storage-usd-tb-month 7.00
   :retail-egress-usd-tb 7.00
   :expansion-factor 2.76
   :node-storage-usd-tb-month 1.50
   :node-egress-usd-tb 2.00
   :read-at "2026-07-29"})

(defn versus-storj
  "kura's retail price against Storj's, and where the difference comes from."
  [rates layout]
  (let [ours (storage-price-usd-tb-month rates layout)
        theirs (:retail-storage-usd-tb-month storj-reference)
        our-cogs (* (v rates :node-storage-usd-tb-month) (multiplier layout))
        their-cogs (* (:node-storage-usd-tb-month storj-reference)
                      (:expansion-factor storj-reference))]
    {:kura-usd-tb-month ours
     :storj-usd-tb-month theirs
     :cheaper-fraction (- 1.0 (/ ours theirs))
     :kura-cogs-usd our-cogs
     :storj-cogs-usd their-cogs
     :kura-margin (- 1.0 (/ our-cogs ours))
     :storj-margin (- 1.0 (/ their-cogs theirs))
     :node-rates-identical? true
     :source-of-difference "the storage multiplier, not the node payout and not the margin"}))

(defn quote-monthly
  "A customer's monthly bill for `logical-tb` stored and `egress-tb` read."
  [rates layout {:keys [logical-tb egress-tb] :or {egress-tb 0}}]
  (let [storage (* logical-tb (storage-price-usd-tb-month rates layout))
        egress (* egress-tb (egress-price-usd-tb rates))]
    {:logical-tb logical-tb
     :egress-tb egress-tb
     :physical-tb (* logical-tb (multiplier layout))
     :storage-usd storage
     :egress-usd egress
     :total-usd (+ storage egress)
     :multiplier (multiplier layout)}))

;; --- node earnings ---------------------------------------------------------

(defn node-earnings
  "What a node earns for holding `physical-tb` and serving `served-tb` over
  one epoch. Nodes are paid for PHYSICAL bytes — the shards actually on
  their disk — which is why the multiplier is the coordinator's cost and not
  the node's concern."
  [rates {:keys [physical-tb served-tb] :or {served-tb 0}}]
  (let [months (/ (double (v rates :epoch-days)) 30.0)
        storage (* physical-tb (v rates :node-storage-usd-tb-month) months)
        egress (* served-tb (v rates :node-egress-usd-tb))]
    {:physical-tb physical-tb
     :served-tb served-tb
     :storage-usd storage
     :egress-usd egress
     :total-usd (+ storage egress)}))

;; --- what tightening the code is worth -------------------------------------

(defn tightening-dividend
  "What moving from `from-layout` to `to-layout` is worth to a customer.

  Phase 0's deliverable, in money. The reduction belongs to the customer as a
  price cut: the measurement that justifies it comes from running their data
  on the network, and a coordinator that keeps the gain has nothing to say
  when asked why anyone should help it measure."
  [rates from-layout to-layout]
  (let [before (storage-price-usd-tb-month rates from-layout)
        after (storage-price-usd-tb-month rates to-layout)]
    {:from-multiplier (multiplier from-layout)
     :to-multiplier (multiplier to-layout)
     :price-before-usd-tb-month before
     :price-after-usd-tb-month after
     :reduction-usd (- before after)
     :reduction-fraction (if (zero? before) 0.0 (/ (- before after) before))}))

(def launch-layout
  "k=16, r=4, g=12, n=32 — multiplier 2.0.

  Conservative on purpose (ADR section 1, open question 1 resolved
  2026-07-29). At launch the node-loss rate is unmeasured, and the costs are
  asymmetric: launching optimistic and finding the rate is worse loses data
  irreversibly, while launching conservative and finding it is better is a
  price cut. So the launch parameter is the one that survives being wrong."
  (lrc/layout {:k 16 :r 4 :g 12}))

(def target-layout
  "k=16, r=4, g=6, n=26 — multiplier 1.625. Where Phase 0's measurement, if
  it supports it, moves the network. This is the only configuration whose
  minimum distance has been established EXHAUSTIVELY (d=8, tolerates 7)."
  (lrc/layout {:k 16 :r 4 :g 6}))

;; --- retail: the quota-tier plane ------------------------------------------
;;
;; Everything above prices a terabyte. Everything below sells a plan. The two
;; do not meet, and that is the point: on the wholesale card a rented backend
;; loses $4.50 on every terabyte it holds, while the same terabyte inside a
;; subscription is 4% of what the subscriber pays for it.

(def default-fx
  "JPY per USD — for REPORTING only.

  The tiers are priced and collected in yen. The rate changes what the margin
  looks like in dollars; it does not change what is earned. So it is
  `:assumed` rather than read: a number that moves every day has no business
  frozen in a library, and a caller holding a real quote passes one in.

  It is also why the margins below are robust. At any rate between 100 and 200
  yen to the dollar the storage cost stays under 8% of revenue, so the
  conclusion does not depend on getting this right."
  {:jpy-per-usd {:value 150.0 :provenance :assumed
                 :note "working figure; revenue is denominated in JPY"}})

(def default-tiers
  "Capacity plans, ADOPTED from kotobase.net rather than derived.

  The free tier's two numbers are that service's live constants, not a
  restatement of them — 512 MiB and 50 pins are what `p2p-byte-quota` and
  `p2p-pin-quota` hold in `net-kotobase`'s `kotobase.plans`, which
  `kotobase.b2` derives its enforced constants from. Taking the paid tiers from
  the same place is a decision about positioning, not a computation, and it is
  recorded as `:adopted` so nobody later reads it as an answer this library
  worked out.

  Those two moved on 2026-07-30 (100 MiB / 3 pins -> 512 MiB / 50 pins,
  net-kotobase ADR-2607301200) to match Neo4j AuraDB Free, and this table
  followed rather than kept quoting a number the service no longer enforces —
  which is the failure mode `:adopted` plus a `:source` exists to make visible.

  The PAID rows are shakier than the free one and are not being changed here:
  the same service restructured to three plans (p2p / secure-managed /
  enterprise, ADR-2607261700) and the yen figures below are its LEGACY tier
  vocabulary, which still resolves but is no longer how it presents itself.
  Re-verify before quoting them anywhere that matters.

  **A quota is a ceiling, not a forecast.** Nothing here predicts what a
  subscriber stores. `tier-economics` prices the ceiling, which makes every
  margin it reports a floor.

  `:regulated` carries no quota because the plan is a contract, not a size.
  Its economics are `:uncomputable` rather than estimated."
  [{:tier :free :jpy-month 0 :quota-bytes 536870912 :quota-objects 50
    :provenance :adopted
    :source "net-kotobase clj-edge/src/kotobase/plans.cljc p2p-byte-quota / p2p-pin-quota (ADR-2607301200), read 2026-07-30"}
   {:tier :standard :jpy-month 2980 :quota-bytes 53687091200 :quota-objects 1000
    :provenance :adopted
    :source "net-kotobase clj-edge/src/kotobase/admin_page.cljc capacity plans, read 2026-07-30 — 'Developer Standard' 50 GiB / 1,000 pins"}
   {:tier :pro :jpy-month 19800 :quota-bytes 536870912000 :quota-objects 10000
    :provenance :adopted
    :source "net-kotobase clj-edge/src/kotobase/admin_page.cljc capacity plans, read 2026-07-30 — 'Team Pro' 500 GiB / 10,000 pins"}
   {:tier :regulated :jpy-month 100000 :quota-bytes nil :quota-objects nil
    :provenance :adopted
    :source "net-kotobase clj-edge/src/kotobase/admin_page.cljc capacity plans, read 2026-07-30 — 'Regulated', dedicated tenant"}])

;; A cost basis is required rather than defaulted, for the same reason
;; `storj.store` requires a clock: the physical rate depends on which
;; providers hold the shards, and this namespace cannot know the fleet mix.
;; Defaulting it here would put a made-up blend behind every margin in the
;; report. `kura.bugyo.budget/cost-basis` builds one from real usage.

(defn worst-case-physical-bytes
  "The most physical storage a subscriber can force a tier to consume.

  Not `quota-bytes x multiplier`. Objects below the replication threshold are
  stored as replicas rather than erasure-coded (three copies against 2.0x), so
  a subscriber who stored nothing but small objects would cost half again as
  much per byte — which is the arithmetic that makes a small-object workload
  look like it breaks the price.

  **It does not, and the object quota is why.** Filling 50 GiB with
  sub-mebibyte objects needs 51,200 of them against a cap of 1,000. The most
  expensive reachable arrangement is therefore every object slot but one spent
  on a just-under-threshold object, with the rest of the byte quota in a single
  large one — and that is only about 1% above the flat coded figure. The pin
  cap that exists for metadata reasons turns out to bound the replication
  exposure too.

  Returns nil when the tier has no quota to price."
  [{:keys [quota-bytes quota-objects]}
   {:keys [coded-multiplier replication-factor replication-threshold-bytes]}]
  (when (and quota-bytes quota-objects (pos? quota-objects))
    (let [;; every slot but one on a small object, the remainder in one large
          small (min (* (dec quota-objects) replication-threshold-bytes) quota-bytes)
          mixed (+ (* small replication-factor)
                   (* (- quota-bytes small) coded-multiplier))
          ;; or spend every slot on a small object and never reach the byte cap
          all-small (* (min (* quota-objects replication-threshold-bytes) quota-bytes)
                       replication-factor)]
      (max mixed all-small))))

(defn tier-economics
  "What one subscription on `tier` earns and costs, at the worst case the
  quota permits.

  `basis` is `{:usd-physical-tb-month :coded-multiplier :replication-factor
  :replication-threshold-bytes}` — the provider bill, not the node payout.
  Renting is the whole point of Phase 0, and the node rate card would price
  storage the fleet is not actually buying at that price.

  **`:storage-gross-margin` covers the provider storage bill and nothing
  else.** Not egress, not the coordinator, not repair bandwidth, not support,
  not the people who build it. It was called `:margin-at-full-quota` for
  exactly as long as it took to try publishing it, at which point a 96% figure
  with no scope on the name was obviously going to be read as a business
  margin. `:cost-excludes` travels with the number so the scope cannot be
  separated from it, and `egress-headroom` prices the largest excluded item.

  It is nil for a free tier: there is no margin on zero revenue, and reporting
  -Inf or 0 would both be claims. What the free tier has is a cost, and it is
  named `:cost-usd-month` like every other tier's."
  [tier basis fx]
  (let [jpy-per-usd (get-in fx [:jpy-per-usd :value])
        revenue (/ (double (:jpy-month tier)) jpy-per-usd)
        physical (worst-case-physical-bytes tier basis)]
    (if (nil? physical)
      {:tier (:tier tier)
       :jpy-month (:jpy-month tier)
       :revenue-usd-month revenue
       :economics :uncomputable
       :why "no quota — this plan is a contract, and its cost follows the contract"}
      (let [logical-tb (/ (double (:quota-bytes tier)) 1e12)
            cost (* (/ physical 1e12) (:usd-physical-tb-month basis))
            flat (* (/ (double (:quota-bytes tier)) 1e12)
                    (:coded-multiplier basis)
                    (:usd-physical-tb-month basis))]
        {:tier (:tier tier)
         :jpy-month (:jpy-month tier)
         :revenue-usd-month revenue
         :quota-bytes (:quota-bytes tier)
         :quota-objects (:quota-objects tier)
         :quota-logical-tb logical-tb
         :worst-case-physical-bytes physical
         :cost-usd-month cost
         :cost-if-all-objects-large-usd flat
         :small-object-penalty-usd (- cost flat)
         :gross-profit-usd-month (- revenue cost)
         :storage-gross-margin (when (pos? revenue) (- 1.0 (/ cost revenue)))
         :cost-excludes ["egress" "coordinator compute" "repair bandwidth"
                         "audit" "support" "development"]
         :implied-usd-logical-tb-month (when (pos? logical-tb) (/ revenue logical-tb))
         :versus-storj-retail-multiple
         (when (pos? logical-tb)
           (/ (/ revenue logical-tb) (:retail-storage-usd-tb-month storj-reference)))}))))

(def default-egress-terms
  "What reading the data costs, by provider.

  B2 gives egress free up to three times the average monthly stored amount and
  charges $0.01/GB beyond it, and its Class A/B/C calls are free — so a
  systematic read touching k shards costs the same in transactions as touching
  one, which is the thing that would otherwise make erasure coding expensive on
  a rented backend.

  Storage is not what threatens a subscription margin. Egress is."
  {:backblaze-b2 {:free-multiple-of-stored 3.0
                  :overage-usd-tb 10.00
                  :transactions "Class A/B/C free; Class D $0.004/10,000 after 2,500/day"
                  :provenance :published
                  :source "backblaze.com/cloud-storage/pricing pay-as-you-go, read 2026-07-30"}})

(defn egress-headroom
  "How much reading a tier's gross profit buys before the margin reaches zero.

  Reported as a multiple of the stored quota, because that is the shape of the
  question an operator actually has — *how many times over may a subscriber
  read what they are storing?* A number in terabytes invites comparing it to
  the storage quota, which is not the same unit of risk.

  Egress is counted in LOGICAL bytes: a systematic read reconstructs nothing,
  so serving a byte moves a byte (ADR section 7). The free allowance, however,
  is a multiple of what the provider is HOLDING, which is the physical figure —
  so the code's multiplier enlarges the free allowance without enlarging the
  bill."
  [economics {:keys [free-multiple-of-stored overage-usd-tb]}]
  (when-let [profit (:gross-profit-usd-month economics)]
    (let [physical-tb (/ (double (:worst-case-physical-bytes economics)) 1e12)
          free-tb (* free-multiple-of-stored physical-tb)
          paid-tb (if (pos? overage-usd-tb) (/ (max 0.0 profit) overage-usd-tb) 0.0)
          total-tb (+ free-tb paid-tb)
          quota-tb (:quota-logical-tb economics)]
      {:free-egress-tb free-tb
       :profit-funded-egress-tb paid-tb
       :breakeven-egress-tb total-tb
       :breakeven-reads-of-full-quota (when (pos? quota-tb) (/ total-tb quota-tb))})))

(defn retail-card
  "The whole retail plane, with the caveats attached to the numbers rather
  than to a document somebody may not read."
  ([basis] (retail-card basis default-tiers default-fx default-egress-terms))
  ([basis tiers fx egress-terms]
   (let [rows (map (fn [t]
                     (let [e (tier-economics t basis fx)]
                       (cond-> e
                         (not= :uncomputable (:economics e))
                         (assoc :egress (egress-headroom e (:backblaze-b2 egress-terms))))))
                   tiers)]
     {:basis :retail
      :cost-basis basis
      :fx fx
      :tiers rows
      :caveats
      ["A quota is a ceiling. Every margin here is priced at the most expensive
        arrangement the quota permits, so it is a floor and not a forecast."
       "Tier prices are ADOPTED from kotobase.net, not derived. They are a
        positioning decision; this library only computes what they cost to serve."
       "The cost basis is the PROVIDER bill, not the node payout rate card. On
        the wholesale basis the same bytes lose money — see
        kura.bugyo.budget/report, which reports that basis and says so."
       "Storage is not the risk to this margin; egress is. See :egress on each
        tier for how many times a subscriber may read their quota before the
        gross profit is gone."
       "The free tier has a cost and no margin. That cost is acquisition, and
        calling it a loss would be a claim about intent rather than arithmetic."
       ":storage-gross-margin covers the provider storage bill only — see
        :cost-excludes on each tier. It is not a business margin and the
        largest excluded item, egress, is priced in :egress."]})))
